               :content {:text "Proscribing P0 (which was missing in the ANSI-SQL deﬁnitions) requires that a transaction T 2 cannot write an object x if an uncommitted transaction T 1 has already modiﬁed x. This is simply a disguised locking deﬁnition, requiring T 1 and T 2 to acquire long write-locks. (Long-term locks are held until the transaction taking them commits; shortterm locks are released immediately after the transaction completes the read or write that triggered the lock attempt.) Similarly, proscribing P1 requires T 1 to acquire a long writelock and T 2 to acquire (at least) a short-term read-lock, and proscribing P2 requires the use of long read and write locks."},
